ean to HOU \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Woody Marks</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>HOU \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>HOU are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Woody Marks</strong> (12 carries, 45 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though HOU's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Nico Collins</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 57 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Dalton Schultz</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 28 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>TEN \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>TEN are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 TEN project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tony Pollard</strong>'s rushing projection (14 carries, 61 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, TEN funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 53 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 24 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 4-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"HOU","col":"#F2405A","players":[{"name":"C.J. Stroud","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":238.51,"book":null,"over_th":225.02,"under_th":250.8,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.33,"book":null,"over_th":0.99,"under_th":1.37,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null}],"td":{"pct":8.5,"fair":"12/1","book":null,"value":null}},{"name":"David Montgomery","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":18.81,"book":null,"over_th":11.19,"under_th":16.77,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Carries","proj":4.47,"book":null,"over_th":3.47,"under_th":4.57,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Rec yards","proj":11.47,"book":null,"over_th":6.28,"under_t